  "briefing": "Turn 210. You are Germany: six walled cities (Munich, Leipzig, Berlin, Cologne, Hamburg and Essen), eight land units (four Spearmen, an Archer, an Infantry and two Artillery) and 189 Gold. Japan, a Modern power with 12 cities, has been at War with you since turn 172. It bought its first Bomber that turn, has struck your units since turn 173 and now has four Bombers; you have no Fighter and no plane, and Flight is researched. In the real game Leipzig fell on turn 211, Cologne on 214, Munich on 216, Berlin on 218, Hamburg on 219 and Essen on 221, which ended Germany. Keep at least four of the six cities through turn 221, which gives you two turns before the first city falls, five before the second and seven before the third, to buy or build Fighters and a garrison. Japan, the human player's seat, replays his recorded orders for the window (his air strikes, rebases and Armour moves, verbatim; any that no longer fit the position are skipped) and the rest of the world, Germany above all, is played by the Hard AI. Japan's own Hard AI bombs but never walks a unit into a stripped city: Germany kept every city in 64 of 64 runs against it, so the offensive has to be his.",
  "tests": "The same air stage as turns 214 and 219 but with time to react: 189 Gold and five turns before Cologne falls. Does the AI see four Bombers' worth of danger in the strikes already landing on its units, and turn its Gold and production into Fighters on guard (one interception each, within four hexes of its hangar) and garrisons? Fighter coverage is the one counter the real game never tried.",
